The Human-English constructs, from an information system \(\mathbf{A}\), a domain \(\mathbf{T}\) of unlabelled binary trees solving \(\mathbf{T}\cong\mathbf{A}+(\mathbf{T}\times\mathbf{T})\), and claims that the solution is identical to the right-hand side because the tokens are the official sum and product tokens: \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}=(\Delta,\Delta)\), atom copies \((X,\Delta)\), and pair copies \((\Delta,(Y,\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}))\) and \((\Delta,(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}},Z))\), the least class satisfying clauses (1)–(3) and the displayed set equation for \(D_{\mathbf{T}}\), which is the only solution under mild assumptions on ordered pairs. Consistency is the least class closed under (4) the empty set, (5) insertion of \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\), (6) the atom copy of a consistent set of \(\mathbf{A}\), and (7) the combined left and right pair copies of consistent sets \(u,v\in\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{T}}\), so that a consistent set, apart from \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\), lies entirely on the atom side or entirely on the product side. Entailment is generated by (8) every set entails \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\), (9) insertion of \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\) preserves entailment, (10) an atom copy of \(w\) entails \((W,\Delta)\) whenever \(w\vdash_{\mathbf{A}}W\), and (11)–(12) the pair copies of \(u\) and \(v\) entail a left pair \((\Delta,(X,\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}))\) whenever \(u\vdash_{\mathbf{T}}X\) and \(v\in\mathrm{Con}_{\mathbf{T}}\), and the symmetric right pair. Scott then says the cases are disjoint except for a trivial overlap of (8) and (9), that checking the information-system axioms is mechanical, and that identity of notation gives \(\mathbf{T}=\mathbf{A}+(\mathbf{T}\times\mathbf{T})\); he further claims that \(\mathbf{T}\) is the least solution in the sense of an initial algebra, that nonstandard models give other solutions, and that the system carries approximable operations \(\mathrm{atom}:\mathbf{A}\to\mathbf{T}\), \(\mathrm{isatom}:\mathbf{T}\to\mathbf{BOOL}\), \(\mathrm{pair}:\mathbf{T}\times\mathbf{T}\to\mathbf{T}\), \(\mathrm{fst}\), and \(\mathrm{snd}\), including infinite elements such as the fixed point \(x=\mathrm{pair}(\mathrm{atom}(a),x)\). The Lean token type `TreeToken` is inductive, with constructors `bot`, `atom`, `pairL`, and `pairR`, and `instDecidableEqTreeToken` makes different constructors distinct, so `pairL` of `treeBot` and `pairR` of `treeBot` are two tokens, whereas clause (3) and the set equation have only one nested pair \((\Delta,(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}},\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}))\). `treeUnfold` sends `bot` to the sum bottom, an atom to the left injection, `pairL t` to the right injection of the product token \((t,\Delta_{\mathbf{T}})\), and `pairR t` to the right injection of \((\Delta_{\mathbf{T}},t)\); `treeUnfold_pairL.bot_eq_pairR_bot` records that the two bottom writings unfold to the same sum token, so the comparison map is not injective and `treeSystem` is not identical to the sum. The numbered `theorem_81`, which is `treeRhs.eq_sum_product`, says only that `treeRhs` equals `sumSystem A (productSystem (treeSystem A) (treeSystem A))`, and both sides are that definition. `TreeCon` has two constructors: a left clause when `atomFinset` is consistent in \(\mathbf{A}\) and both `pairLFinset` and `pairRFinset` are empty, and a right clause when `atomFinset` is empty and both pair projections are themselves consistent. That matches (6) and (7); `TreeCon_empty` is the left clause on the empty set, and `TreeCon_insert_bot` is (5), because `atomFinset_insert_bot`, `pairLFinset_insert_bot`, and `pairRFinset_insert_bot` leave the three projections unchanged. `TreeCon.pairs_empty_of_left` and `TreeCon.atoms_empty_of_right` are the separation into an atom copy or a product copy. The projections do not reproduce Definition 6.1 on the shared product bottom: `pairLFinset_singleton_pairR` and `pairRFinset_singleton_pairL` are empty, so `pairR` of `treeBot` contributes \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\) only to the right projection, while the product token \((\Delta_{\mathbf{T}},\Delta_{\mathbf{T}})\) that `treeUnfold` assigns to it contributes \(\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\) to both `fstFinset` and `sndFinset`. `TreeEntPayload` repairs entailment of that bottom only. It holds of `bot` outright, which is (8); of an atom only when `atomFinset` is nonempty and \(\mathbf{A}\) entails that atom, so the empty set does not entail \(\mathrm{atom}(\Delta_{\mathbf{A}})\) even though (10) allows \(w=\emptyset\) and \(W=\Delta_{\mathbf{A}}\); and of `pairL t` when the atom projection is empty, both pair projections are consistent, and either the left projection is nonempty and carries the payload of \(t\), or \(t\) is `bot` and the right projection is nonempty, with the symmetric clause for `pairR`. Those extra bottom disjuncts make a nonempty pair side entail both writings of the product bottom, which is what Definition 6.3(iv'') requires and what literal (11)–(12) fail to require when \(u=v=\emptyset\) and \(X=\Delta_{\mathbf{T}}\). Clause (9) is not a generator: inserting `bot` does not change the projections. The axiom proof is the one Scott calls mechanical. `TreeCon_subset` inducts on the consistency constructor and uses monotonicity of the three projections. `TreeCon_singleton` inducts on the token. `TreeCon_insert_of_ent` inducts on the entailed token, applying the entailment-consistency axiom on an atom and, on a pair, either the inductive hypothesis or insertion of bottom into a consistent pair projection. `TreeEntPayload.of_mem` and `TreeEnt.of_mem` give reflexivity. `TreeEntPayload_trans` inducts on the token: the bottom case is immediate; an atom is cut in \(\mathbf{A}\) after every atom of the intermediate projection is entailed; a left pair either transports the inner payload along left-pair entailments, using `pairL_ne_or_subset_bot` and `eq_singleton_bot_of_subset` when the intermediate left projection collapses to the singleton of bottom, or uses a nonempty right projection for the bottom disjunct, and the right-pair case is symmetric. `TreeEnt_trans` pairs that payload step with consistency of the stronger set, and `treeSystem` assembles the six axioms. Nothing in the closure proves that \(D_{\mathbf{T}}\) is the unique solution of the set equation, that \(\mathbf{T}\) is initial, that nonstandard models yield further solutions, or that \(\mathrm{atom}\), \(\mathrm{isatom}\), \(\mathrm{pair}\), \(\mathrm{fst}\), and \(\mathrm{snd}\) exist, so the infinite list \(x=\mathrm{pair}(\mathrm{atom}(a),x)\) is not obtained. **Theorem:** partial — `treeSystem` is an information system whose consistency and entailment follow the sum of \(\mathbf{A}\) with a product of tree tokens, but \(\mathbf{T}\) is not identical to \(\mathbf{A}+(\mathbf{T}\times\mathbf{T})\), because `pairL treeBot` and `pairR treeBot` stay distinct while `treeUnfold` identifies them, and `theorem_81` is only the definitional equality of `treeRhs` with that sum. **Proof:** partial — the six axiom checks follow Scott’s case split, with the nonempty-projection side conditions of Definition 6.3 written into `TreeEntPayload` where clauses (10)–(12) do not have them, while identity of the two sides, initiality, and the \(S\)-expression operations are not proved.
